podman — rootful containers inside the sandbox

  Run nested containers as root with podman:

    podman run --rm hello-world
    podman build -t myimage .

  The sandbox shell is non-root, so `podman` is a wrapper that elevates
  via sudo (the real binary is /usr/bin/podman). Containers run as real
  root in their own namespaces — no uid mapping — which avoids the
  rootless limitations that block some images and mounts.

  Images and layers persist across csb runs under your home volume.

  Note: rootful nested containers can't get cgroup delegation or mount a
  private /proc in an unprivileged container, so csb runs the sandbox as a
  privileged container (the docker:dind recipe) when this addon is enabled.
  That is a large privilege relaxation — use the podman-rootless addon if
  you don't need the rootful behaviour.

  With the systemd addon also enabled, the Podman API (Docker-compatible
  REST) service comes up automatically on the standard rootful socket,
  reachable by the sandbox user without sudo. Talk to it with the real
  binary, not the `podman` wrapper above — sudo strips CONTAINER_HOST (and
  DOCKER_HOST) via env_reset, so passing it to the wrapper silently runs
  local root podman instead of exercising the socket:

    /usr/bin/podman --url unix:///run/podman/podman.sock info
    curl --unix-socket /run/podman/podman.sock http://d/v5.0.0/libpod/_ping

  docker-compose, testcontainers, and other Docker-API clients read
  DOCKER_HOST rather than CONTAINER_HOST:

    export DOCKER_HOST=unix:///run/podman/podman.sock
    docker-compose up

  To skip that export entirely, enable the addon as:

    --addon "podman docker-sock"

  which also publishes the API at the Docker default path
  (/var/run/docker.sock), where clients look when DOCKER_HOST is unset —
  so `docker.from_env()` and friends work with no configuration at all.
  It is opt-in because that path asserts podman *is* the Docker daemon for
  everything in the container.

  Access is group-gated to the sandbox user, not world-open — see the
  comment in entrypoint.sh for why that's no more privilege than the
  sudoers rule already grants.
